Returning a stack reference from an ``@safe`` function is now an error under ``-preview=fastdfa``

A function declared ``@safe`` may not return a value that refers to the storage
of one of its own local variables. That was already an error with
``-preview=dip1000``; with ``-preview=fastdfa`` the check no longer needs that
flag.

---
@safe:
int[] viaVariable()
{
    int[3] arr = [1, 2, 3];
    int[] s = arr[];
    return s;
} // error: Stack variable exceeds its lifetime by being returned
---

The reference does not have to be produced by the return statement itself: the
same error is reported when it comes back through a local variable, when the
slice is cut again (`s[0 .. 2]`), or when the address of an element of a
static array local is taken
($(LINK2 https://github.com/dlang/dmd/issues/20508, #20508)).

``@system`` and ``@trusted`` functions may still return references to their own
frame. The check stays within one function at a time: escapes that travel
through a call, for example through
$(LINK2 https://github.com/dlang/dmd/issues/23878, #23878), are not reported
yet.

Behavior without ``-preview=fastdfa`` is unchanged.
